00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:24.055
&gt;&gt; Hi, everyone.
Thank you for coming.

00:00:24.056 --> 00:00:26.715
Today, we're going to have
Professor Torsten Hoefler

00:00:26.716 --> 00:00:28.805
present some of his work.

00:00:28.806 --> 00:00:32.030
So, Torsten has been
a professor at UIUC,

00:00:32.031 --> 00:00:34.645
and now, he's a professor
at ETH Zurich.

00:00:34.646 --> 00:00:38.905
He is visiting the Quantum group
here in Microsoft,

00:00:38.906 --> 00:00:43.185
and he works primarily on
parallel computing performance,

00:00:43.186 --> 00:00:45.810
and he's published
over 200 papers,

00:00:45.811 --> 00:00:47.555
which is quite impressive.

00:00:47.556 --> 00:00:50.310
Donald had pointed me to Torsten

00:00:50.311 --> 00:00:53.370
and I haven't been
aware of his work,

00:00:53.371 --> 00:00:55.060
so it sounds very interesting.

00:00:55.061 --> 00:00:56.570
So I'm looking
forward to learning

00:00:56.571 --> 00:00:58.610
about his work today. Thank you.

00:00:58.611 --> 00:01:00.185
&gt;&gt; Very good. Thank
you very much.

00:01:00.186 --> 00:01:01.900
Yes, I'm from ETH,

00:01:01.901 --> 00:01:03.405
and I've been here two months,

00:01:03.406 --> 00:01:04.625
and this is actually today

00:01:04.626 --> 00:01:06.625
my last day as
a visiting researcher,

00:01:06.626 --> 00:01:08.010
but it's likely I will come back.

00:01:08.011 --> 00:01:10.400
So, if you want to talk to
me then either catch me

00:01:10.401 --> 00:01:13.280
right after the talk or later.

00:01:13.281 --> 00:01:15.710
This is, in fact, work
that has been done with

00:01:15.711 --> 00:01:18.755
many people almost centrally

00:01:18.756 --> 00:01:21.440
my student Maciej has
contributed to the development

00:01:21.441 --> 00:01:24.425
of the Slim Fly topology that
I will be talking about.

00:01:24.426 --> 00:01:26.540
But at the beginning, I want
to give a quick overview

00:01:26.541 --> 00:01:28.285
of network topologies,

00:01:28.286 --> 00:01:29.860
in general, what
the development was.

00:01:29.861 --> 00:01:31.730
But let me start with

00:01:31.731 --> 00:01:33.465
a quick statement
about data centers.

00:01:33.466 --> 00:01:34.840
Microsoft is operating in

00:01:34.841 --> 00:01:36.430
a pretty sizable data
center business,

00:01:36.431 --> 00:01:38.720
and it seems to be
moving further into

00:01:38.721 --> 00:01:40.680
the central attention of

00:01:40.681 --> 00:01:43.010
the company as we
are all observing.

00:01:43.011 --> 00:01:44.600
There are some observations we

00:01:44.601 --> 00:01:45.610
can make about data centers.

00:01:45.611 --> 00:01:47.955
So, first, Google,

00:01:47.956 --> 00:01:50.320
some people from
Google, so Dennis Abts,

00:01:50.321 --> 00:01:53.525
he actually observed in
this paper down here that

00:01:53.526 --> 00:01:56.300
about up to 50 percent of
the energy consumption of

00:01:56.301 --> 00:01:57.770
a data center could be somewhat

00:01:57.771 --> 00:01:59.440
related to the networking
infrastructure.

00:01:59.441 --> 00:02:00.800
So, he saw various components in

00:02:00.801 --> 00:02:03.750
the networking infrastructure
and other things.

00:02:03.751 --> 00:02:05.930
Furthermore, there
is another work that

00:02:05.931 --> 00:02:08.320
actually calculates that
up to 33 percent of

00:02:08.321 --> 00:02:10.795
the network- 33 percent of

00:02:10.796 --> 00:02:14.540
the data center costs could
be the networking components.

00:02:14.541 --> 00:02:16.070
So, this is something we have

00:02:16.071 --> 00:02:17.830
to see with a grain
of salt because,

00:02:17.831 --> 00:02:19.615
of course, that varies
on the exact setting.

00:02:19.616 --> 00:02:21.980
So, I believe there is
a very large spend.

00:02:21.981 --> 00:02:23.620
I would say here, it's somewhere

00:02:23.621 --> 00:02:26.155
between 20 percent
and this 50 percent,

00:02:26.156 --> 00:02:29.045
it feels very high, and
then the 33 percent,

00:02:29.046 --> 00:02:31.620
that could also be lower but
it could also be higher.

00:02:31.621 --> 00:02:32.795
So, that really depends on

00:02:32.796 --> 00:02:34.690
how well your data
center is connected.

00:02:34.691 --> 00:02:36.680
But if you look at the past

00:02:36.681 --> 00:02:39.405
of these data center networks,
what happened there?

00:02:39.406 --> 00:02:43.360
I mean, what happened from
the early 1980s until today?

00:02:43.361 --> 00:02:44.615
This is something we built on,

00:02:44.616 --> 00:02:48.895
so I want to quickly explain
what was the history here.

00:02:48.896 --> 00:02:50.900
In the early days, data center

00:02:50.901 --> 00:02:53.005
topologies or, at this time,

00:02:53.006 --> 00:02:54.900
computer topologies,
so nobody talked

00:02:54.901 --> 00:02:56.915
about data centers
in the early 1980s,

00:02:56.916 --> 00:02:59.660
that term wasn't
probably even coined.

00:02:59.661 --> 00:03:02.494
These early topologies
were mainly restricted

00:03:02.495 --> 00:03:05.150
by the amount of silicon
available on a single chip.

00:03:05.151 --> 00:03:08.200
So, what that meant, they had
to have a very small radix.

00:03:08.201 --> 00:03:10.765
So, when I draw
these graphs here,

00:03:10.766 --> 00:03:12.840
you just read them
like a normal graph,

00:03:12.841 --> 00:03:15.990
and every vertex is a
switch in the network,

00:03:15.991 --> 00:03:17.520
and every line is a connection,

00:03:17.521 --> 00:03:18.945
so a cable in the network.

00:03:18.946 --> 00:03:20.690
What we can see here is that for

00:03:20.691 --> 00:03:22.250
this particular network topology,

00:03:22.251 --> 00:03:24.170
we choose the dimensionality
of the network,

00:03:24.171 --> 00:03:25.830
so in this case, it's two.

00:03:25.831 --> 00:03:27.130
Then the radix, so the number of

00:03:27.131 --> 00:03:29.070
ports per switch we need
to support is four.

00:03:29.071 --> 00:03:30.560
All right, so that's
extremely simple.

00:03:30.561 --> 00:03:31.985
It's very, very
simple to construct.

00:03:31.986 --> 00:03:33.680
This is actually
the main reason why

00:03:33.681 --> 00:03:35.950
these networks have
survived for so long.

00:03:35.951 --> 00:03:37.630
In fact, if you pump
this up a little bit,

00:03:37.631 --> 00:03:40.335
if you go to a three or four
or five-dimensional tori,

00:03:40.336 --> 00:03:42.410
you can get a rather
reasonable network,

00:03:42.411 --> 00:03:43.890
but, of course, the
radix is growing.

00:03:43.891 --> 00:03:45.620
But there are two problems
with this network,

00:03:45.621 --> 00:03:47.690
and these are actually
the two fundamental parameters

00:03:47.691 --> 00:03:49.675
we care about in networking,

00:03:49.676 --> 00:03:51.730
one is the latency and
the other one is the bandwidth.

00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:53.360
So, with latency, I really mean

00:03:53.361 --> 00:03:55.780
the distance traveling from
one vertex to another.

00:03:55.781 --> 00:03:57.940
The maximum distance here
is somehow the Dth root

00:03:57.941 --> 00:03:59.330
of the total number of vertices

00:03:59.331 --> 00:04:00.665
in the network, so that's bad.

00:04:00.666 --> 00:04:02.275
So, it's actually very bad.

00:04:02.276 --> 00:04:03.815
The bandwidth is even worse

00:04:03.816 --> 00:04:05.180
because you can cut
this network very

00:04:05.181 --> 00:04:08.580
easily by removing
links from the network,

00:04:08.581 --> 00:04:10.510
so the global bandwidth
I'm talking about here,

00:04:10.511 --> 00:04:13.200
that's relatively
bad in this effort.

00:04:13.201 --> 00:04:16.020
Nevertheless, since they
are very easy to construct,

00:04:16.021 --> 00:04:17.325
many HPC networks,

00:04:17.326 --> 00:04:18.770
so high-performance
computing networks,

00:04:18.771 --> 00:04:20.870
have used that topology to scale

00:04:20.871 --> 00:04:23.120
very far because people
call this network scalable

00:04:23.121 --> 00:04:25.580
of this topology because you
can just add more rows and

00:04:25.581 --> 00:04:28.395
more columns in
your particular topology,

00:04:28.396 --> 00:04:29.950
and if you want to build
a very large computer,

00:04:29.951 --> 00:04:31.115
that's a very good thing.

00:04:31.116 --> 00:04:33.500
By the way, there is
a fundamental disagreement

00:04:33.501 --> 00:04:34.940
between me and

00:04:34.941 --> 00:04:36.500
the rest of the world
or the rest of

00:04:36.501 --> 00:04:39.065
the networking architects when
we talk about scalability.

00:04:39.066 --> 00:04:41.620
I do not consider this
network as scalable.

00:04:41.621 --> 00:04:44.710
So, by scalability,
when I say that term,

00:04:44.711 --> 00:04:47.600
I mean that the
bandwidth scales and

00:04:47.601 --> 00:04:50.505
the latency scales as we add
more vertices to a network.

00:04:50.506 --> 00:04:52.400
In this sense, this
network is not scalable.

00:04:52.401 --> 00:04:54.680
However, every network architect,

00:04:54.681 --> 00:04:56.395
when the network
architect says scalable,

00:04:56.396 --> 00:04:58.700
they usually mean, "We can
add more stuff to it and

00:04:58.701 --> 00:05:01.130
it's relatively easy to
rewire the network."

00:05:01.131 --> 00:05:03.650
In that definition,
it is scalable.

00:05:03.651 --> 00:05:05.120
So, what happened
then, well, this was,

00:05:05.121 --> 00:05:07.835
of course, discussed
and discovered.

00:05:07.836 --> 00:05:10.730
So, then we went to
hypercube style networks

00:05:10.731 --> 00:05:12.020
and there the benefit is that we

00:05:12.021 --> 00:05:13.600
could actually have
a logarithmic latency,

00:05:13.601 --> 00:05:15.750
so that's very good,
and a full bandwidth.

00:05:15.751 --> 00:05:17.000
So, now, we have N
divided by two and

00:05:17.001 --> 00:05:18.995
we've got rid of
the square root term.

00:05:18.996 --> 00:05:21.725
So, we essentially
have enough bandwidth

00:05:21.726 --> 00:05:23.630
that half of the nodes can talk

00:05:23.631 --> 00:05:25.010
to the other half of
the nodes without

00:05:25.011 --> 00:05:26.330
any congestion in the network

00:05:26.331 --> 00:05:28.150
and not just the
square root of those.

00:05:28.151 --> 00:05:30.705
Of course, the network
radix is not logarithmic,

00:05:30.706 --> 00:05:32.180
so that is fundamentally

00:05:32.181 --> 00:05:34.190
limiting your size of the
network you could have at

00:05:34.191 --> 00:05:37.010
the end because you will
still need to build a switch

00:05:37.011 --> 00:05:38.570
with N ports and then you can

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:40.280
only have two to the N endpoints,

00:05:40.281 --> 00:05:42.045
and N was very
small at that time.

00:05:42.046 --> 00:05:44.955
So, what people developed, well,

00:05:44.956 --> 00:05:47.645
the first idea to use were,

00:05:47.646 --> 00:05:51.120
well, a brief idea,
were tree networks.

00:05:51.121 --> 00:05:53.065
So, people just looked at
trees and this is what

00:05:53.066 --> 00:05:55.655
many data centers
still look today,

00:05:55.656 --> 00:05:57.840
the kind of the last
generation data centers.

00:05:57.841 --> 00:05:59.555
Look, the tree networks.

00:05:59.556 --> 00:06:01.990
&gt;&gt; Sorry, even before,

00:06:01.991 --> 00:06:03.280
can you just define
what bandwidth,

00:06:03.281 --> 00:06:04.720
latency and radix mean in this.

00:06:04.721 --> 00:06:06.235
&gt;&gt; Okay, sure. So, you mean here?

00:06:06.236 --> 00:06:08.365
&gt;&gt; Yes, or in any
of them because-.

00:06:08.366 --> 00:06:10.440
&gt;&gt; So, the latency is really

00:06:10.441 --> 00:06:12.550
the maximum distance, actually,

00:06:12.551 --> 00:06:14.180
the distance of a
shortest path from

00:06:14.181 --> 00:06:16.390
any vertex to any other vertex
in the network.

00:06:16.391 --> 00:06:18.940
So, here in this case it's a
log base two N. All right.

00:06:18.941 --> 00:06:22.575
So, if you have N vertices,
the maximum distance,

00:06:22.576 --> 00:06:24.680
which is the
shortest path between

00:06:24.681 --> 00:06:28.480
the maximum separate two
vertices is log base two N.

00:06:28.481 --> 00:06:30.290
&gt;&gt; The shortest or the largest?

00:06:30.291 --> 00:06:32.910
&gt;&gt; That is the shortest path,
not the longest path.

00:06:32.911 --> 00:06:33.900
&gt;&gt; Between any?

00:06:33.901 --> 00:06:35.150
&gt;&gt; Between any vertices, right.

00:06:35.151 --> 00:06:37.925
Then the bandwidth is really

00:06:37.926 --> 00:06:40.700
how much bandwidth
can I carry through

00:06:40.701 --> 00:06:43.180
the network in terms
of N. All right.

00:06:43.181 --> 00:06:44.810
So, if I'm, for example,
I have a simple

00:06:44.811 --> 00:06:46.930
all-to-all pattern everybody
sends to everybody else,

00:06:46.931 --> 00:06:49.760
how much bandwidth can the
network accept in that case?

00:06:49.761 --> 00:06:51.860
So, you want this to be N, right?

00:06:51.861 --> 00:06:54.145
So, and in the previous one,

00:06:54.146 --> 00:06:57.560
this was somewhat square root N
to the D, so very little.

00:06:57.561 --> 00:07:00.160
We want this to be N and
this is, here in this case,

00:07:00.161 --> 00:07:01.450
this is perfect
because we are very

00:07:01.451 --> 00:07:03.710
close to N in some way.

00:07:03.711 --> 00:07:06.600
The radix is basically your
technology parameter where,

00:07:06.601 --> 00:07:08.570
how large of a switch
you need to build in

00:07:08.571 --> 00:07:10.940
order to support that
network topology.

00:07:10.941 --> 00:07:12.810
&gt;&gt; So, for example, in this case,

00:07:12.811 --> 00:07:16.950
is it easy to see why it's log N?

00:07:16.951 --> 00:07:19.750
&gt;&gt; This is just a
hypercube, so this is-.

00:07:19.751 --> 00:07:21.825
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I guess I don't know
what the switch is here.

00:07:21.826 --> 00:07:23.200
&gt;&gt; Well, a switch is a vertex.

00:07:23.201 --> 00:07:24.410
&gt;&gt; All right, okay. So, it's like

00:07:24.411 --> 00:07:26.455
the number of edges
leaving a vertex?

00:07:26.456 --> 00:07:28.080
&gt;&gt; That's the radix, exactly.

00:07:28.081 --> 00:07:28.555
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:07:28.556 --> 00:07:30.460
&gt;&gt; As you grow
the hypercube, you will

00:07:30.461 --> 00:07:32.350
have more neighbors, I mean,

00:07:32.351 --> 00:07:34.040
every dimension you
add to the hypercube,

00:07:34.041 --> 00:07:35.640
you will double
the number of neighbors,

00:07:35.641 --> 00:07:39.100
and this is where you have
the logarithmic impact here.

00:07:39.101 --> 00:07:41.230
Okay, cool. So, and then we have

00:07:41.231 --> 00:07:43.410
this tree network and everybody
knows the tree, right?

00:07:43.411 --> 00:07:44.870
The bandwidth is one
because we all need to

00:07:44.871 --> 00:07:46.910
go through the root,
so that's bad.

00:07:46.911 --> 00:07:48.810
The latency is two log N

00:07:48.811 --> 00:07:50.460
because we need to
go up and down,

00:07:50.461 --> 00:07:52.690
all right, so, because a tree
has logarithmic depth.

00:07:52.691 --> 00:07:54.360
The radix is two because we
have only two neighbors.

00:07:54.361 --> 00:07:56.090
We should have probably
started with this one from

00:07:56.091 --> 00:07:57.440
an educational
perspective because

00:07:57.441 --> 00:07:59.095
that's definitely
the easiest one.

00:07:59.096 --> 00:08:01.460
However, this is not
how people build

00:08:01.461 --> 00:08:04.280
these trees because usually
in a data center context,

00:08:04.281 --> 00:08:06.350
we have so-called top-of-the-rack
switches and then we

00:08:06.351 --> 00:08:08.540
have backbone switches
in the sense that

00:08:08.541 --> 00:08:12.200
the network technology used
for implementing these cables

00:08:12.201 --> 00:08:13.670
gets usually beefier towards

00:08:13.671 --> 00:08:16.180
the root and has
just a higher bandwidth.

00:08:16.181 --> 00:08:19.185
All right, in HPC or actually,
in modern technologies,

00:08:19.186 --> 00:08:20.720
we don't really do this

00:08:20.721 --> 00:08:22.580
because we want to

00:08:22.581 --> 00:08:24.340
have the fastest connection
to the endpoint.

00:08:24.341 --> 00:08:25.430
So, if we already take

00:08:25.431 --> 00:08:26.830
our fastest connection
to the endpoint,

00:08:26.831 --> 00:08:30.350
we don't have anything
else to do to the top,

00:08:30.351 --> 00:08:32.465
so we just have that link speed

00:08:32.466 --> 00:08:33.925
that we want to get
to the endpoint.

00:08:33.926 --> 00:08:36.870
Okay, so under that assumption,

00:08:36.871 --> 00:08:38.200
what was developed,

00:08:38.201 --> 00:08:39.690
that was actually
developed quite early on,

00:08:39.691 --> 00:08:41.930
this is the so-called
Clos network

00:08:41.931 --> 00:08:43.710
or fat-tree network there,

00:08:43.711 --> 00:08:46.330
not isomorphic but
very, very, very close.

00:08:46.331 --> 00:08:47.630
So, we can discuss
the differences,

00:08:47.631 --> 00:08:48.665
but they're usually minor.

00:08:48.666 --> 00:08:49.910
So, this is a Clos network,

00:08:49.911 --> 00:08:50.930
this is a fat-tree network,

00:08:50.931 --> 00:08:52.250
this is a folded Clos network,

00:08:52.251 --> 00:08:54.260
but they all have
the same principle.

00:08:54.261 --> 00:08:56.565
The idea is that they
have a fixed radix

00:08:56.566 --> 00:09:00.170
and then they grow multiple
layers with this fixed radix,

00:09:00.171 --> 00:09:03.325
which gives us a two log N
latency, right,

00:09:03.326 --> 00:09:05.750
and that also gives

00:09:05.751 --> 00:09:07.875
us a full bandwidth in
that particular case.

00:09:07.876 --> 00:09:09.770
So, that's somewhat
the dream topology.

00:09:09.771 --> 00:09:12.770
In fact, this is the topology
that is used for

00:09:12.771 --> 00:09:14.960
most of the installations

00:09:14.961 --> 00:09:16.685
of large supercomputer
systems today.

00:09:16.686 --> 00:09:19.580
It is used in many modern
data centers today as well.

00:09:19.581 --> 00:09:22.190
So, that's kind of the
the default topology.

00:09:22.191 --> 00:09:24.665
However, in this talk, I will
tell you how to actually

00:09:24.666 --> 00:09:25.970
build a topology with

00:09:25.971 --> 00:09:28.805
very similar properties
but has half the cost,

00:09:28.806 --> 00:09:31.140
because we still
quantify constants.

00:09:31.141 --> 00:09:32.350
Asymptotically, this topology is

00:09:32.351 --> 00:09:33.790
optimal, if you don't
care about constants.

00:09:33.791 --> 00:09:35.315
But, of course, in
real life, if you actually

00:09:35.316 --> 00:09:37.430
invest a couple of 100
million into a data center,

00:09:37.431 --> 00:09:39.140
then you care about
a constant of two,

00:09:39.141 --> 00:09:40.840
I hope, most people do.

00:09:40.841 --> 00:09:42.680
Then, there is this fancy,

00:09:42.681 --> 00:09:45.310
Kautz topology, that
is, in fact, a graph.

00:09:45.311 --> 00:09:47.360
Now, we are going to talk a
little bit more about graphs.

00:09:47.361 --> 00:09:51.275
We're abstracting this away
from the actual technology.

00:09:51.276 --> 00:09:54.280
Whatever we implement
these switches with and that was

00:09:54.281 --> 00:09:57.100
used in the HPC contexts
that is a directed graph.

00:09:57.101 --> 00:09:58.655
So, all graphs before were

00:09:58.656 --> 00:10:01.040
undirected and directed graphs

00:10:01.041 --> 00:10:03.365
so we could kind of
need directed cables.

00:10:03.366 --> 00:10:04.890
I've not seen directed cables

00:10:04.891 --> 00:10:06.790
before whatever was
constructed for this and it's

00:10:06.791 --> 00:10:08.740
technologically feasible
was constructed for

00:10:08.741 --> 00:10:11.190
this particular machine,
the ciphertext machine.

00:10:11.191 --> 00:10:14.180
Unfortunately, the startup
company went belly up

00:10:14.181 --> 00:10:17.395
pretty quickly after
they developed this.

00:10:17.396 --> 00:10:20.860
Parts of the reason could be,

00:10:20.861 --> 00:10:23.200
that this was an incredibly
complex network to route and

00:10:23.201 --> 00:10:26.175
actually to understand
but we will see.

00:10:26.176 --> 00:10:27.330
The nice thing here is

00:10:27.331 --> 00:10:29.165
that this is actually
hitting a lower bound.

00:10:29.166 --> 00:10:32.990
So, this is nearly
strictly optimal.

00:10:32.991 --> 00:10:34.990
So, if we go now
from the 1980's to

00:10:34.991 --> 00:10:37.930
2005 this is the quick summary
I just gave you.

00:10:37.931 --> 00:10:40.330
But at that area and
around that time

00:10:40.331 --> 00:10:41.410
two fundamental change has

00:10:41.411 --> 00:10:43.360
happened in the
networking business.

00:10:43.361 --> 00:10:45.220
So, one fundamental change
is that we went from

00:10:45.221 --> 00:10:48.345
copper cables to
fiber enabled cables.

00:10:48.346 --> 00:10:50.500
At that time suddenly
fiber became affordable.

00:10:50.501 --> 00:10:51.860
It was around for a long time

00:10:51.861 --> 00:10:53.775
but it became
a mass market product.

00:10:53.776 --> 00:10:55.370
What does fiber change
in the business?

00:10:55.371 --> 00:10:58.640
Fiber changes that we actually
can go long distance.

00:10:58.641 --> 00:11:00.185
So, suddenly we were not bound

00:11:00.186 --> 00:11:02.160
by locality constraints anymore.

00:11:02.161 --> 00:11:04.280
These copper cables if
you know there are only

00:11:04.281 --> 00:11:06.925
five meters long if you run
them at a high frequency.

00:11:06.926 --> 00:11:08.760
The fiber cables
there a kilometer

00:11:08.761 --> 00:11:10.370
long and basically you can wire

00:11:10.371 --> 00:11:11.920
your datacenter
twice in a row and

00:11:11.921 --> 00:11:13.935
still have a single cable
so that's perfectly fine.

00:11:13.936 --> 00:11:16.340
That was change number one
we enabled long cables.

00:11:16.341 --> 00:11:17.690
Change number two is that we went

00:11:17.691 --> 00:11:19.060
from small ratings which is

00:11:19.061 --> 00:11:20.500
too high ratings
switches and that's

00:11:20.501 --> 00:11:21.985
just simply following
Moore's Law.

00:11:21.986 --> 00:11:23.570
We had more transistors
available on

00:11:23.571 --> 00:11:25.330
the chip stability
switches and then

00:11:25.331 --> 00:11:28.740
we could just build switches
with larger radiuses.

00:11:28.741 --> 00:11:30.310
So, we went from

00:11:30.311 --> 00:11:32.260
these four ports
switches to today

00:11:32.261 --> 00:11:34.855
easily 100 ports switches
on a single chip.

00:11:34.856 --> 00:11:36.595
That's extremely important.

00:11:36.596 --> 00:11:38.150
Both these developments taken

00:11:38.151 --> 00:11:41.010
together lead to
the development of

00:11:41.011 --> 00:11:43.620
the dragonfly topology which

00:11:43.621 --> 00:11:46.370
there were two competing designs,

00:11:46.371 --> 00:11:48.620
I was part of one designs
so one design actually

00:11:48.621 --> 00:11:49.830
survived the other one didn't of

00:11:49.831 --> 00:11:51.425
course I was betting
on the wrong horse.

00:11:51.426 --> 00:11:52.980
So, I was working with IBM and

00:11:52.981 --> 00:11:54.675
the so-called parks topology.

00:11:54.676 --> 00:11:56.090
Wonderful idea there was

00:11:56.091 --> 00:11:57.350
only one machine built

00:11:57.351 --> 00:11:59.335
and I'm not allowed
to talk about this.

00:11:59.336 --> 00:12:01.820
Has a very one
large-scale machine

00:12:01.821 --> 00:12:03.260
builds and I'm not allowed
to talk about this.

00:12:03.261 --> 00:12:06.290
However, very similar topology

00:12:06.291 --> 00:12:09.460
was used by Cray to
build the Cascade Style,

00:12:09.461 --> 00:12:11.660
area style network, that

00:12:11.661 --> 00:12:14.310
is where we currently have
the largest installation in

00:12:14.311 --> 00:12:16.730
Switzerland with about 5,000 end

00:12:16.731 --> 00:12:19.260
nodes running
that particular topology.

00:12:19.261 --> 00:12:20.940
The idea here is that we have

00:12:20.941 --> 00:12:23.000
groups that are fully connected.

00:12:23.001 --> 00:12:25.200
So, we see five groups here,

00:12:25.201 --> 00:12:28.090
and then these groups form
so-called super switches

00:12:28.091 --> 00:12:29.570
or super groups that

00:12:29.571 --> 00:12:31.030
themselves are again
fully connected.

00:12:31.031 --> 00:12:33.845
So, it's a two-layer
fully connected network.

00:12:33.846 --> 00:12:36.845
Typically, we use
electrical connections

00:12:36.846 --> 00:12:38.745
within the group and
optical connections

00:12:38.746 --> 00:12:40.370
outside of the group.
That was the idea.

00:12:40.371 --> 00:12:43.555
That's the network that came
from engineering constraints

00:12:43.556 --> 00:12:47.400
and here we get again an
approximately "Full bandwidth."

00:12:47.401 --> 00:12:49.300
So, we're losing a factor of
two here I can talk about

00:12:49.301 --> 00:12:51.615
it it's not too bad to lose
this factor of two in fact.

00:12:51.616 --> 00:12:54.000
We get a fixed latency
so three to five,

00:12:54.001 --> 00:12:55.580
that also means that
our maximum size

00:12:55.581 --> 00:12:56.930
of the network is limited.

00:12:56.931 --> 00:12:58.510
So, the maximum size of a cascade

00:12:58.511 --> 00:13:00.060
system that you can buy from

00:13:00.061 --> 00:13:03.340
Cray is about 65,000 nodes
same for the parks topology,

00:13:03.341 --> 00:13:05.570
we can't buy more
with those switches.

00:13:05.571 --> 00:13:09.130
However, that's enough
for many installations.

00:13:09.131 --> 00:13:10.330
There's also a fixed Radix

00:13:10.331 --> 00:13:11.850
node that we are talking about.

00:13:11.851 --> 00:13:14.230
But now if you take this,

00:13:14.231 --> 00:13:17.910
we took all the experience
we made with that topology,

00:13:17.911 --> 00:13:19.780
all the other previous
topologists were

00:13:19.781 --> 00:13:22.750
designed with technology in mind.

00:13:22.751 --> 00:13:24.770
So, we're ingenious engineers

00:13:24.771 --> 00:13:27.270
sitting there and trying
to understand how to

00:13:27.271 --> 00:13:29.580
use the new technology
constraints in order

00:13:29.581 --> 00:13:32.680
to build the next
network topology.

00:13:32.681 --> 00:13:34.720
What we did as scientists.

00:13:34.721 --> 00:13:36.390
So, I was always working
with engineers because

00:13:36.391 --> 00:13:38.145
I really enjoy working
with engineers.

00:13:38.146 --> 00:13:39.320
But we have a slightly different

00:13:39.321 --> 00:13:40.580
viewpoints so what we did,

00:13:40.581 --> 00:13:42.490
is we said, "Okay, let's
take this to the extreme.

00:13:42.491 --> 00:13:45.670
Let's just assume we have
an all fiber network because

00:13:45.671 --> 00:13:47.155
fiber is relatively cheap

00:13:47.156 --> 00:13:49.715
today if you choose
the right transceivers.

00:13:49.716 --> 00:13:52.080
Let's assume we want

00:13:52.081 --> 00:13:54.095
to build the cheapest
possible network."

00:13:54.096 --> 00:13:56.810
What I mean by cheap as we
want to have a full bandwidth.

00:13:56.811 --> 00:14:00.515
So, bandwidth of
proportional to N.

00:14:00.516 --> 00:14:03.160
We want to have
the lowest possible latency

00:14:03.161 --> 00:14:05.240
and the lowest possible
radix at that time.

00:14:05.241 --> 00:14:07.330
So, what happened
then, and we use

00:14:07.331 --> 00:14:09.860
mathematical optimization
principles to

00:14:09.861 --> 00:14:13.020
achieve this so this is
really the key here.

00:14:13.021 --> 00:14:14.570
But what we found is that

00:14:14.571 --> 00:14:16.415
actually if we drive
this optimization,

00:14:16.416 --> 00:14:18.575
all we need to care
about is the diameter.

00:14:18.576 --> 00:14:20.170
It took me quite a while
to understand this

00:14:20.171 --> 00:14:23.270
but after all it is
really really intuitive.

00:14:23.271 --> 00:14:24.980
If you have a network
where you run

00:14:24.981 --> 00:14:27.430
your traffic through and you

00:14:27.431 --> 00:14:28.880
have basically all to

00:14:28.881 --> 00:14:31.700
all communication the further
you go in this network,

00:14:31.701 --> 00:14:33.595
the more links you traverse,

00:14:33.596 --> 00:14:36.090
the more bandwidth you
need on those links.

00:14:36.091 --> 00:14:37.990
If you go very short you need

00:14:37.991 --> 00:14:39.980
less bandwidth thus
you need less cables.

00:14:39.981 --> 00:14:42.340
That's the intuition
behind this whole idea.

00:14:42.341 --> 00:14:45.825
Now the question is. Well,
it's basically written here.

00:14:45.826 --> 00:14:47.750
What we found is that in order to

00:14:47.751 --> 00:14:49.710
get to the lower bound we

00:14:49.711 --> 00:14:51.380
can improve 50 percent over

00:14:51.381 --> 00:14:53.710
fat tree and one-third
over dragonfly.

00:14:53.711 --> 00:14:56.350
So which means we can
make these topologies at

00:14:56.351 --> 00:14:59.725
the same performance
by a factor of two,

00:14:59.726 --> 00:15:00.800
less expensive or by

00:15:00.801 --> 00:15:02.265
a factor of one-third
less expensive.

00:15:02.266 --> 00:15:04.830
So now, let me tell you, yes.

00:15:04.831 --> 00:15:05.310
&gt;&gt; The diameter?

00:15:05.311 --> 00:15:07.360
&gt;&gt; Oh, the diameter is again,

00:15:07.361 --> 00:15:09.605
the path length

00:15:09.606 --> 00:15:12.920
so that's between any two
nodes the minimum path.

00:15:12.921 --> 00:15:17.080
You want to shrink
the graph as a whole.

00:15:17.081 --> 00:15:19.030
Though that's the same ceilings.

00:15:19.031 --> 00:15:19.400
&gt;&gt; Oh, it is.

00:15:19.401 --> 00:15:20.530
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Yeah. So, I actually

00:15:20.531 --> 00:15:22.585
confused latency early,
this is interchangeable.

00:15:22.586 --> 00:15:24.450
But the idea is that if
you want to make sure that

00:15:24.451 --> 00:15:27.465
any node can reach any other
node in the network

00:15:27.466 --> 00:15:29.060
with no more in this case

00:15:29.061 --> 00:15:33.655
than two or four nodes
or intermediate cables.

00:15:33.656 --> 00:15:36.855
That's the key idea and
let me look at an example.

00:15:36.856 --> 00:15:39.040
So, we have an example that's
a full bandwidth fat tree.

00:15:39.041 --> 00:15:40.395
This is in fact a machine

00:15:40.396 --> 00:15:42.020
that has been deployed
in Japan that's

00:15:42.021 --> 00:15:45.260
a TSUBAME2.0 machine that's
a three-level fat tree.

00:15:45.261 --> 00:15:48.195
So, just another fat tree
we have the switches,

00:15:48.196 --> 00:15:49.810
three levels, level one,

00:15:49.811 --> 00:15:51.890
level two, and level three.

00:15:51.891 --> 00:15:54.660
We have the cabling as
I showed before is I

00:15:54.661 --> 00:15:57.375
think 24 or 36 port switches
I'm not so sure.

00:15:57.376 --> 00:15:58.620
Half of the cables going down,

00:15:58.621 --> 00:16:01.420
half the cables going up
very simple topology.

00:16:01.421 --> 00:16:03.705
This is a diameter for topology.

00:16:03.706 --> 00:16:07.635
We can create a similar setting

00:16:07.636 --> 00:16:09.390
with a diameter two
topology that gives us

00:16:09.391 --> 00:16:11.430
50 percent less routers and

00:16:11.431 --> 00:16:15.375
30 percent fewer cables
in this particular case.

00:16:15.376 --> 00:16:18.515
The cables will be slightly
longer though, but fiber,

00:16:18.516 --> 00:16:20.730
the cost of fiber as we will
see in a couple of minutes

00:16:20.731 --> 00:16:23.535
is actually not too
much for per meter,

00:16:23.536 --> 00:16:25.125
it's a lot per transceiver.

00:16:25.126 --> 00:16:26.710
So, you pay a lot overhead

00:16:26.711 --> 00:16:28.685
per cable because you
need two transceiver,

00:16:28.686 --> 00:16:30.830
but per meter is
relatively cheap.

00:16:30.831 --> 00:16:33.430
Okay. So, let me now
explain how we built this.

00:16:33.431 --> 00:16:35.730
So, this was just the
motivation so far.

00:16:35.731 --> 00:16:37.550
But before we do this, I want to

00:16:37.551 --> 00:16:39.500
explain you this Moore Bound.

00:16:39.501 --> 00:16:41.580
So, the idea is now, well,

00:16:41.581 --> 00:16:43.010
if we now want to minimize

00:16:43.011 --> 00:16:46.065
the diameter for
a given switch degree.

00:16:46.066 --> 00:16:48.005
So, we have the technology,

00:16:48.006 --> 00:16:50.220
we have a switch that
we have constructed.

00:16:50.221 --> 00:16:52.530
Let's say that switch
has 15 ports,

00:16:52.531 --> 00:16:54.685
and now we want to build

00:16:54.686 --> 00:16:58.480
the graph that connects
the maximum number of endpoints,

00:16:58.481 --> 00:17:01.110
using let's say diameter
two or diameter

00:17:01.111 --> 00:17:05.760
three that can be constructed
with a 15 port switch.

00:17:05.761 --> 00:17:07.780
So, there is in
fact an upper bound

00:17:07.781 --> 00:17:08.800
that's really easy to prove.

00:17:08.801 --> 00:17:10.710
So, I'm going to prove
this upper bound

00:17:10.711 --> 00:17:13.075
in the next five minutes
that's called a Moore Bound.

00:17:13.076 --> 00:17:15.790
The idea here is in graph
theoretical terms that is

00:17:15.791 --> 00:17:17.680
an upper bound on
the number of vertices

00:17:17.681 --> 00:17:19.770
in a graph with a given diameter.

00:17:19.771 --> 00:17:23.099
So, maximum minimum path
between any two vertices,

00:17:23.100 --> 00:17:24.820
maximum minimum length path

00:17:24.821 --> 00:17:27.910
and a given radix so
the maximum degree,

00:17:27.911 --> 00:17:29.760
but for our purpose we choose

00:17:29.761 --> 00:17:30.890
the same degree for every

00:17:30.891 --> 00:17:32.550
single vertex so it
is a regular graph.

00:17:32.551 --> 00:17:34.465
So, in this Moore bound it
is very easy to construct.

00:17:34.466 --> 00:17:38.260
Basically say well, if
we have zero diameter.

00:17:38.261 --> 00:17:40.220
Well, then we can reach
exactly one node.

00:17:40.221 --> 00:17:43.265
It's pretty clear, if
the degree is k. Well,

00:17:43.266 --> 00:17:45.040
how many more nodes can
be reached in one step?

00:17:45.041 --> 00:17:46.945
Well, that's obviously one plus

00:17:46.946 --> 00:17:50.410
k. If you have
the degree the diameter,

00:17:50.411 --> 00:17:54.360
if you allowed the diameter
two we can reach at most,

00:17:54.361 --> 00:17:56.520
each of those can reach
at most k plus one

00:17:56.521 --> 00:17:59.235
more and so on.

00:17:59.236 --> 00:18:01.500
So, now we can easily show

00:18:01.501 --> 00:18:04.390
this Moore Bound and it's
really the sum where it ranges

00:18:04.391 --> 00:18:06.920
from the k times i equals

00:18:06.921 --> 00:18:09.820
zero to D minus one then
k minus one to the i.

00:18:09.821 --> 00:18:11.775
So, that's by construction

00:18:11.776 --> 00:18:13.670
that's the maximum number
of nodes we can reach.

00:18:13.671 --> 00:18:14.820
In fact that is not tight.

00:18:14.821 --> 00:18:16.240
The bound is very far from

00:18:16.241 --> 00:18:18.125
tight but it is an upper bound.

00:18:18.126 --> 00:18:19.850
This is in fact the best
known upper bound for

00:18:19.851 --> 00:18:22.410
this graph and looking at

00:18:22.411 --> 00:18:24.080
these large graphs is

00:18:24.081 --> 00:18:27.090
a very interesting
theoretical construct.

00:18:27.091 --> 00:18:28.420
So, you can in fact can you get

00:18:28.421 --> 00:18:30.205
your graph named after you.

00:18:30.206 --> 00:18:31.560
So, one of the famous graphs that

00:18:31.561 --> 00:18:33.605
hits the Moore bound
is the Petersen graph.

00:18:33.606 --> 00:18:36.175
The other graph I showed you
the Hoffman Singleton graph.

00:18:36.176 --> 00:18:39.440
There's only one other
non-trivial graph I

00:18:39.441 --> 00:18:41.080
believe it's 57 vertices

00:18:41.081 --> 00:18:42.850
or something but it
has to be checked,

00:18:42.851 --> 00:18:43.920
that achieves that Moore

00:18:43.921 --> 00:18:45.900
bound that graph
has not been found.

00:18:45.901 --> 00:18:48.090
So, if you find it you can
get it named after you.

00:18:48.091 --> 00:18:49.930
Their whole there's
actually if you Google for

00:18:49.931 --> 00:18:51.330
the degree diameter problem

00:18:51.331 --> 00:18:53.605
this is a very
well-known problem in

00:18:53.606 --> 00:18:55.990
Wikipedia there's a page with

00:18:55.991 --> 00:18:58.590
people's names and their
graph constructions

00:18:58.591 --> 00:18:59.700
that hit the upper

00:18:59.701 --> 00:19:02.310
bound because people
do- mathematicians have fun with

00:19:02.311 --> 00:19:03.900
this they tried to really find

00:19:03.901 --> 00:19:05.620
the largest possible graph.

00:19:05.621 --> 00:19:06.950
We are not going
to do this because

00:19:06.951 --> 00:19:08.430
if you look at this table it is

00:19:08.431 --> 00:19:10.790
a double exponential problem

00:19:10.791 --> 00:19:13.405
to build these
graphs exhaustively,

00:19:13.406 --> 00:19:14.810
and if you look at this we

00:19:14.811 --> 00:19:16.145
see that we don't get very far.

00:19:16.146 --> 00:19:18.150
So, we use another construction

00:19:18.151 --> 00:19:20.100
in order to build our graph.

00:19:20.101 --> 00:19:22.285
However, we can proof
for that construction

00:19:22.286 --> 00:19:24.980
that it is within 10 percent
of the Moore bound.

00:19:24.981 --> 00:19:27.550
So, you can gain at
most 10 percent.

00:19:27.551 --> 00:19:30.920
If you've done any engineering
10 percent is in the vice,

00:19:30.921 --> 00:19:32.820
so that's- that's fine.

00:19:32.821 --> 00:19:34.640
Let me tell you a very high

00:19:34.641 --> 00:19:35.960
level picture of how that works,

00:19:35.961 --> 00:19:37.150
how we construct such a graph.

00:19:37.151 --> 00:19:39.100
And this now involves:
first of all I give you

00:19:39.101 --> 00:19:40.150
a high-level picture
and then I'll give you

00:19:40.151 --> 00:19:41.510
a little bit more
detailed picture,

00:19:41.511 --> 00:19:42.780
because it involved group theory.

00:19:42.781 --> 00:19:44.030
So, it's not totally

00:19:44.031 --> 00:19:45.930
trivial but it's also
not complicated.

00:19:45.931 --> 00:19:50.535
So, we want to build a
graph of diameter two.

00:19:50.536 --> 00:19:52.245
Why do we choose diameter two?

00:19:52.246 --> 00:19:54.385
Well, first of
all diameter three,

00:19:54.386 --> 00:19:57.345
we have done with this
graph I showed you before,

00:19:57.346 --> 00:19:58.740
the dragonfly graph or

00:19:58.741 --> 00:20:00.890
the PERC's topologies is
a diameter three graph.

00:20:00.891 --> 00:20:02.345
Because we have groups

00:20:02.346 --> 00:20:04.210
and we have at
most one hop in the group,

00:20:04.211 --> 00:20:05.440
at most one intra-group hop,

00:20:05.441 --> 00:20:07.815
and at most one hop
in the target group.

00:20:07.816 --> 00:20:09.825
So, this is the
diameter three graph,

00:20:09.826 --> 00:20:11.245
we have such a construction.

00:20:11.246 --> 00:20:13.375
For diameter two it's
more complicated,

00:20:13.376 --> 00:20:15.055
because we don't have such a

00:20:15.056 --> 00:20:16.335
close to optimal construction.

00:20:16.336 --> 00:20:19.600
Diameter one is a useless
graph because diameter of one

00:20:19.601 --> 00:20:21.250
means I have a single switch in

00:20:21.251 --> 00:20:23.440
the middle that needs to
connect all nodes, right.

00:20:23.441 --> 00:20:25.330
So, that's not going to work
because I will not build

00:20:25.331 --> 00:20:27.365
a switch for
technology constraints,

00:20:27.366 --> 00:20:31.535
not build a single chip switch
with 100,000 end points,

00:20:31.536 --> 00:20:32.825
right. That's not
going to happen.

00:20:32.826 --> 00:20:34.810
So, how does our
construction look like?

00:20:34.811 --> 00:20:36.650
It's based on this mathematical

00:20:36.651 --> 00:20:37.860
theory developed
here and they call

00:20:37.861 --> 00:20:40.700
them MMS graphs
based on the names,

00:20:40.701 --> 00:20:41.940
Mckay, Miller and Siran.

00:20:41.941 --> 00:20:44.400
So, we have two
identical groups of

00:20:44.401 --> 00:20:47.700
routers that will be
the construction.

00:20:47.701 --> 00:20:50.605
So, each of these dark squares

00:20:50.606 --> 00:20:52.990
is a router in the
network or a switch,

00:20:52.991 --> 00:20:54.855
yet again same terminology.

00:20:54.856 --> 00:20:58.035
Then, these switches or
routers form groups.

00:20:58.036 --> 00:20:59.635
So, here in this case,
it's a five by five.

00:20:59.636 --> 00:21:00.970
So there are five groups of

00:21:00.971 --> 00:21:03.490
five nodes each,
in the left side,

00:21:03.491 --> 00:21:05.605
and these groups have
identical connections,

00:21:05.606 --> 00:21:07.010
and I will explain you
how these connections

00:21:07.011 --> 00:21:08.740
come to place in
a couple of minutes.

00:21:08.741 --> 00:21:11.020
On the right side, we
have exactly the same but

00:21:11.021 --> 00:21:13.020
a different identical connection.

00:21:13.021 --> 00:21:15.725
So, we have five groups
each of those is identical,

00:21:15.726 --> 00:21:18.970
but they have a different
communication pattern.

00:21:18.971 --> 00:21:21.130
And then this is still
the very high level picture.

00:21:21.131 --> 00:21:22.805
Then, each of those groups

00:21:22.806 --> 00:21:24.380
is connected to the other side,

00:21:24.381 --> 00:21:26.280
but it's connected
to all the groups.

00:21:26.281 --> 00:21:29.340
As you can see, it feels a
little bit like a dragonfly.

00:21:29.341 --> 00:21:32.015
Like I have shown
you before, the 2D,

00:21:32.016 --> 00:21:34.800
the two level fully
connected graph.

00:21:34.801 --> 00:21:37.210
So, here each of these groups
and the left graph is

00:21:37.211 --> 00:21:38.370
connected to all of the groups on

00:21:38.371 --> 00:21:40.615
the right side and
vice versa of course.

00:21:40.616 --> 00:21:45.130
So, it's a very high connectivity
pattern, okay, good.

00:21:45.131 --> 00:21:48.080
So, let me now explain a little
bit more how to, in fact,

00:21:48.081 --> 00:21:51.295
build a real network like
this and it starts like

00:21:51.296 --> 00:21:55.295
much of a group theory by
selecting a prime number and,

00:21:55.296 --> 00:21:56.975
finding a prime power, right.

00:21:56.976 --> 00:21:59.895
I see some people smiling
and enjoying group theory.

00:21:59.896 --> 00:22:01.695
So, we like it, we
select the prime power.

00:22:01.696 --> 00:22:04.210
The nice thing is for computer
scientists or engineers.,

00:22:04.211 --> 00:22:06.855
two is a prime number
and any power of

00:22:06.856 --> 00:22:08.440
two will work to construct

00:22:08.441 --> 00:22:10.615
such a graph, usually like that.

00:22:10.616 --> 00:22:13.430
We will not use two as
an example, but fine.

00:22:13.431 --> 00:22:15.700
And there's a theorem
that we can show that

00:22:15.701 --> 00:22:19.090
any prime power Q can be
decomposed into 4W plus Delta.

00:22:19.091 --> 00:22:21.185
So, you have to find
this decomposition,

00:22:21.186 --> 00:22:21.960
let us also find,

00:22:21.961 --> 00:22:23.500
just believe it, in

00:22:23.501 --> 00:22:24.850
case you're not familiar
with that theorem,

00:22:24.851 --> 00:22:26.815
it's a very basic theorem.

00:22:26.816 --> 00:22:28.790
Let me build a slim fly based on

00:22:28.791 --> 00:22:31.575
this prime power Q that we found,

00:22:31.576 --> 00:22:34.220
and this Q is exactly here.

00:22:34.221 --> 00:22:35.480
So, in this case,

00:22:35.481 --> 00:22:38.630
Q equals five, five is
a prime number as we know,

00:22:38.631 --> 00:22:42.490
and we get obviously
five by five twice,

00:22:42.491 --> 00:22:48.435
so 2Q square many routers
in that network, okay.

00:22:48.436 --> 00:22:50.040
So, that's the
graph construction.

00:22:50.041 --> 00:22:51.680
And the network radix, you can

00:22:51.681 --> 00:22:54.170
compute by using
this Delta from up here.

00:22:54.171 --> 00:22:56.735
But, that then again we will
see in a couple of minutes.

00:22:56.736 --> 00:22:58.140
Once we have picked
that prime power,

00:22:58.141 --> 00:23:01.165
we have to build
so-called finite fields,

00:23:01.166 --> 00:23:02.570
and again, if you
like group theory

00:23:02.571 --> 00:23:04.400
you will immediately
relate to this.

00:23:04.401 --> 00:23:06.210
And the idea of
this finite field,

00:23:06.211 --> 00:23:08.465
if Q is prime that's
easiest case.

00:23:08.466 --> 00:23:10.200
So, for example in
five we can do this.

00:23:10.201 --> 00:23:11.910
The finite field we'll
just have all the elements

00:23:11.911 --> 00:23:13.755
from zero to Q minus one.

00:23:13.756 --> 00:23:15.280
So zero, one, two, three,

00:23:15.281 --> 00:23:16.970
four in this case, okay?

00:23:16.971 --> 00:23:19.320
With modular arithmetic modular

00:23:19.321 --> 00:23:21.040
five in all these cases, okay.

00:23:21.041 --> 00:23:23.010
For example if you look
at this Q equals five,

00:23:23.011 --> 00:23:25.630
we get two times
five square routers.

00:23:25.631 --> 00:23:28.165
So, that's 50 Network
ratings will be seven,

00:23:28.166 --> 00:23:30.320
and so here it is five by five

00:23:30.321 --> 00:23:33.055
and the finite field will
go from zero to four.

00:23:33.056 --> 00:23:35.455
Very, very, very
simple. Okay cool.

00:23:35.456 --> 00:23:37.310
So, now let's
actually look at how

00:23:37.311 --> 00:23:39.575
we can label these routers
we will need this later.

00:23:39.576 --> 00:23:42.130
So, now we have
these 50 routers here and now

00:23:42.131 --> 00:23:44.720
we can arrange them in
a very simple labeling space,

00:23:44.721 --> 00:23:47.820
we can first have fully
Q equals five example,

00:23:47.821 --> 00:23:50.435
we can name the left graph

00:23:50.436 --> 00:23:52.640
zero and the right
graph one, okay.

00:23:52.641 --> 00:23:55.040
And then we can of
course introduce

00:23:55.041 --> 00:23:58.500
this finite field sub Q times
finite field sub Q, right.

00:23:58.501 --> 00:24:00.250
So, we name those guys

00:24:00.251 --> 00:24:02.360
five by five and it's

00:24:02.361 --> 00:24:04.800
a very simple three-dimensional
naming scheme, okay.

00:24:04.801 --> 00:24:08.300
So, is there
a question? All right.

00:24:08.301 --> 00:24:10.115
So that's an extremely
simple scheme

00:24:10.116 --> 00:24:11.685
that we will get back later too.

00:24:11.686 --> 00:24:14.825
But now, once we have
found our prime power,

00:24:14.826 --> 00:24:17.770
we have constructed
all finite field,

00:24:17.771 --> 00:24:20.165
for each finite field,
we can find

00:24:20.166 --> 00:24:22.840
the so-called primitive element
in the finite field.

00:24:22.841 --> 00:24:24.720
So, primitive element
is an element in

00:24:24.721 --> 00:24:27.050
the finite field that
generates a finite field.

00:24:27.051 --> 00:24:29.375
So, that's again a tiny bit
of group theory.

00:24:29.376 --> 00:24:31.550
So, what it really does,

00:24:31.551 --> 00:24:33.090
is that all non-zero elements of

00:24:33.091 --> 00:24:35.980
the finite field can be

00:24:35.981 --> 00:24:37.470
constructed by taking

00:24:37.471 --> 00:24:39.240
that primitive element
to some power,

00:24:39.241 --> 00:24:41.550
modulo the size of
the finite field.

00:24:41.551 --> 00:24:43.800
So, we can basically write

00:24:43.801 --> 00:24:47.610
everything as this Psi
i to the i, right.

00:24:47.760 --> 00:24:50.910
So, in this case for five,

00:24:50.911 --> 00:24:52.200
what we can do is we

00:24:52.201 --> 00:24:54.750
find this primitive
element equals two.

00:24:54.751 --> 00:24:56.290
Now, the typical question
is, how do you

00:24:56.291 --> 00:24:58.320
find the primitive element
of a finite field.

00:24:58.321 --> 00:25:02.285
Yes, we don't know.
However, there is one,

00:25:02.286 --> 00:25:03.720
we know that there is one,

00:25:03.721 --> 00:25:06.700
and for all the constructions
that we have done so far,

00:25:06.701 --> 00:25:08.450
it's really easy to
find via brute force,

00:25:08.451 --> 00:25:10.330
you just try, we

00:25:10.331 --> 00:25:11.980
have a small Python script
that finds this.

00:25:11.981 --> 00:25:15.155
There are some shortcuts to
finding primitive elements,

00:25:15.156 --> 00:25:17.630
but it's not even worth
doing it so we just

00:25:17.631 --> 00:25:20.645
brute force the thing,
that's very simple.

00:25:20.646 --> 00:25:22.640
And as you can see, because

00:25:22.641 --> 00:25:24.160
the finite fields
don't have to get very

00:25:24.161 --> 00:25:25.790
large until we reach 200,000

00:25:25.791 --> 00:25:27.715
or a million end points
in the network.

00:25:27.716 --> 00:25:31.115
So, we don't need
these large finite fields.

00:25:31.116 --> 00:25:34.240
You can easily check that
two is a primitive element,

00:25:34.241 --> 00:25:35.860
I don't want to do it with
you because you have to check

00:25:35.861 --> 00:25:38.785
every single of
those four values here.

00:25:38.786 --> 00:25:43.390
For example, if you look at this

00:25:43.391 --> 00:25:48.025
two to the four mod five
is basically the same,

00:25:48.026 --> 00:25:49.925
and it's one, right.

00:25:49.926 --> 00:25:54.170
So, two to the four
16.5 is one, good.

00:25:54.171 --> 00:25:56.760
What we do then, and
this is also very

00:25:56.761 --> 00:25:59.300
well known in
the field group theory,

00:25:59.301 --> 00:26:00.495
we build the set of

00:26:00.496 --> 00:26:02.730
even powers and
the set of odd powers.

00:26:02.731 --> 00:26:04.130
This is the set of even powers,

00:26:04.131 --> 00:26:05.135
this is the set of odd powers.

00:26:05.136 --> 00:26:06.930
And again, this is
a very mechanical thing,

00:26:06.931 --> 00:26:08.230
you don't need to
understand anything,

00:26:08.231 --> 00:26:09.610
you just apply it.

00:26:09.611 --> 00:26:11.270
And here, we have these two sets,

00:26:11.271 --> 00:26:12.560
x and x primes, again,

00:26:12.561 --> 00:26:15.170
the even powers is to the zero,

00:26:15.171 --> 00:26:16.630
to the two, and so on,

00:26:16.631 --> 00:26:18.655
and then the odd powers.

00:26:18.656 --> 00:26:20.920
Again, once we have
these two fields here,

00:26:20.921 --> 00:26:22.400
in this case it's one
and four and two and

00:26:22.401 --> 00:26:24.435
three, still very simple,

00:26:24.436 --> 00:26:27.225
we can use these fields to build

00:26:27.226 --> 00:26:30.650
the connections for
these groups, okay?

00:26:30.651 --> 00:26:33.550
So, and this is also
a relatively simple rule.

00:26:33.551 --> 00:26:34.810
So, we first look at the set of

00:26:34.811 --> 00:26:37.740
the odd powers which

00:26:37.741 --> 00:26:39.560
build our connections
for sub graph zero,

00:26:39.561 --> 00:26:41.150
and the set of
the even powers build

00:26:41.151 --> 00:26:42.695
our connections
for sub graph one.

00:26:42.696 --> 00:26:44.285
Again, if you go
through the example,

00:26:44.286 --> 00:26:45.395
we look at the routers,

00:26:45.396 --> 00:26:46.770
we look at one of these column,

00:26:46.771 --> 00:26:48.405
and we look at our set,

00:26:48.406 --> 00:26:51.330
one and four, and now we connect

00:26:51.331 --> 00:26:52.540
all routers that have a vertical

00:26:52.541 --> 00:26:54.280
Manhattan distance of
the element in the set.

00:26:54.281 --> 00:26:56.175
So, first we look at
one, while we connect

00:26:56.176 --> 00:26:59.350
every router that has
a distance one, right.

00:26:59.351 --> 00:27:00.830
So it's relatively simple.

00:27:00.831 --> 00:27:02.665
And then we look at four.

00:27:02.666 --> 00:27:04.020
Well there are only

00:27:04.021 --> 00:27:05.845
two routers that have
a distance four,

00:27:05.846 --> 00:27:09.190
which are the top and the bottom
one, then we're done.

00:27:09.191 --> 00:27:11.395
We do this for all
the groups on the left side,

00:27:11.396 --> 00:27:13.435
we apply two three

00:27:13.436 --> 00:27:15.455
for all the groups on
the right side and we are done.

00:27:15.456 --> 00:27:18.680
This is our intra-group
connection, okay?

00:27:18.681 --> 00:27:19.920
And obviously, this is not

00:27:19.921 --> 00:27:21.140
a real network we need to connect

00:27:21.141 --> 00:27:23.715
the groups among
each other as well.

00:27:23.716 --> 00:27:25.265
So, the intra-group connection,

00:27:25.266 --> 00:27:27.585
and there we have
a simple linear rule,

00:27:27.586 --> 00:27:28.950
the two routers, zero, X,

00:27:28.951 --> 00:27:30.855
Y and one M, C are connected,

00:27:30.856 --> 00:27:33.300
if and only if Y
equals MX plus C. So,

00:27:33.301 --> 00:27:35.110
again a very simple
rule to evaluate,

00:27:35.111 --> 00:27:36.870
you can write a very small
script that just builds

00:27:36.871 --> 00:27:39.195
these graphs so we have
lots of these scripts.

00:27:39.196 --> 00:27:40.990
And if you look at an example,

00:27:40.991 --> 00:27:42.550
we take this router
one, zero,zero.

00:27:42.551 --> 00:27:43.610
So, here X is zero,

00:27:43.611 --> 00:27:47.540
Y is zero and M and
C, is already here,

00:27:47.541 --> 00:27:48.695
M and C is zero, but you can

00:27:48.696 --> 00:27:50.500
exchange those and that would

00:27:50.501 --> 00:27:53.900
connect this router to all
these routers back here, okay?

00:27:53.901 --> 00:27:55.785
If you just pick another example,

00:27:55.786 --> 00:27:57.490
the one, one, zero router.

00:27:57.491 --> 00:27:59.575
So, that guy would connect

00:27:59.576 --> 00:28:02.480
to the diagonal of the rhombus.

00:28:02.481 --> 00:28:04.630
It becomes an interesting
structure, but,

00:28:04.631 --> 00:28:08.240
that's due to these prime
fields, these finite fields.

00:28:08.241 --> 00:28:10.550
They make it actually a
quite pretty, in fact,

00:28:10.551 --> 00:28:12.600
if you look at
those graphs at the end.

00:28:12.601 --> 00:28:14.800
And then, I don't want to
do all examples because

00:28:14.801 --> 00:28:17.405
it gets not so pretty if
you plot them on a 2D,

00:28:17.406 --> 00:28:19.320
a very small 2D space.

00:28:19.321 --> 00:28:22.405
So, it's a very tightly
connected network at the end,

00:28:22.406 --> 00:28:25.180
and this construction
guarantees, and so,

00:28:25.181 --> 00:28:27.080
I'll get to it in a minute
that we only have

00:28:27.081 --> 00:28:30.015
one intermediate hop from
getting from A to B,

00:28:30.016 --> 00:28:32.950
right, and this construction
also guarantees,

00:28:32.951 --> 00:28:34.155
which is not so easy to show,

00:28:34.156 --> 00:28:36.055
that we are within
10% of the lower bound.

00:28:36.056 --> 00:28:37.490
And so that can be
proven but that's

00:28:37.491 --> 00:28:39.780
nothing for this talk.
So, we need to talk.

00:28:39.781 --> 00:28:42.125
&gt;&gt; So, how much is the radix?

00:28:42.126 --> 00:28:46.640
&gt;&gt; The radix is this 3Q
minus four over something.

00:28:46.641 --> 00:28:50.500
So, that's also related to
the prime power you choose.

00:28:50.730 --> 00:28:52.820
Now the questions that we have,

00:28:52.821 --> 00:28:54.915
built the network of switches.

00:28:54.916 --> 00:28:56.745
We have connected
all the switches,

00:28:56.746 --> 00:28:59.440
but of course switches are
useless without servers.

00:28:59.441 --> 00:29:01.510
So, how do we now put
these servers into place?

00:29:01.511 --> 00:29:03.420
This is a very simple question,

00:29:03.421 --> 00:29:06.235
how many endpoints do we
attach to each router?

00:29:06.236 --> 00:29:08.360
So, here we have a switch,

00:29:08.361 --> 00:29:10.650
we have the cables going into

00:29:10.651 --> 00:29:12.915
the network and here we have
the number of endpoints.

00:29:12.916 --> 00:29:14.840
So, this is everything up

00:29:14.841 --> 00:29:17.010
here is the slim flight topology,

00:29:17.011 --> 00:29:20.940
everything down here is just
our use of that topology.

00:29:20.941 --> 00:29:22.580
The rule is relatively

00:29:22.581 --> 00:29:24.200
simple if we want
full global bandwidth.

00:29:24.201 --> 00:29:26.685
So, what is full
global bandwidth mean?

00:29:26.686 --> 00:29:29.720
It's basically if you run
an all to all steady state,

00:29:29.721 --> 00:29:33.475
which means each of those nodes
or end points down here,

00:29:33.476 --> 00:29:37.370
injects packets as fast as
it can into the network,

00:29:37.371 --> 00:29:38.830
and addresses those packets to

00:29:38.831 --> 00:29:40.685
random end points in the network.

00:29:40.686 --> 00:29:42.870
That's the workload we
want to support just

00:29:42.871 --> 00:29:45.285
random global all-to-all
communication.

00:29:45.286 --> 00:29:48.575
We want to achieve that
each of those endpoints,

00:29:48.576 --> 00:29:50.590
can do this at the full line rate

00:29:50.591 --> 00:29:52.460
as much as this link supports.

00:29:52.461 --> 00:29:54.080
By the way this link
is as fast as

00:29:54.081 --> 00:29:55.770
any link in the network.

00:29:55.771 --> 00:29:57.695
So, there's a homogeneous

00:29:57.696 --> 00:30:00.235
link speed network in this case.

00:30:00.236 --> 00:30:03.120
So, all of these guys shoot
as fast as they can to

00:30:03.121 --> 00:30:06.250
all other nodes and the rule
is relatively simple,

00:30:06.251 --> 00:30:07.780
so here's again the animation

00:30:07.781 --> 00:30:09.855
and the rule is
relatively simple.

00:30:09.856 --> 00:30:12.170
So, we can compute this I'm
slightly running out of time

00:30:12.171 --> 00:30:14.690
so let me just give
you the result,

00:30:14.691 --> 00:30:17.430
the ideas and the way
you can derive this

00:30:17.431 --> 00:30:20.290
the intuition is
again that we have,

00:30:20.291 --> 00:30:22.630
do we have an animation
for this exactly.

00:30:22.631 --> 00:30:24.920
So, that we put one-third of

00:30:24.921 --> 00:30:28.180
the links from the router

00:30:28.181 --> 00:30:30.040
that leave the router
to end points,

00:30:30.041 --> 00:30:31.495
and two-thirds to the network.

00:30:31.496 --> 00:30:33.075
Why is it two-thirds
to the network?

00:30:33.076 --> 00:30:37.450
Well, remember we have
the distance to network.

00:30:37.451 --> 00:30:40.720
So, this means these one-third,

00:30:40.721 --> 00:30:42.580
they will shoot into the network,

00:30:42.581 --> 00:30:43.960
and they will leave,

00:30:43.961 --> 00:30:46.515
they will occupy one-third
of the outgoing links,

00:30:46.516 --> 00:30:48.880
just by shooting
into the network.

00:30:48.881 --> 00:30:50.450
But then there are also

00:30:50.451 --> 00:30:53.070
these other nodes that
need to route through

00:30:53.071 --> 00:30:54.670
my router because
they have to have

00:30:54.671 --> 00:30:57.335
an intermediate hop
and get out again.

00:30:57.336 --> 00:30:59.010
This is again
one-third incoming and

00:30:59.011 --> 00:31:01.235
one-third outgoing, so
that's the intuition.

00:31:01.236 --> 00:31:03.305
If you have a diameter
three network by the way,

00:31:03.306 --> 00:31:06.540
this would be one quarter
at the end, and so on.

00:31:06.541 --> 00:31:09.600
If you assume uniform
routing and we will get

00:31:09.601 --> 00:31:11.085
