00:43:26.316 --> 00:43:27.715
Then, we have the Slim Fly,

00:43:27.716 --> 00:43:31.795
which is here very close and
contender for the Fat Tree.

00:43:31.796 --> 00:43:34.310
Okay? So, these are the networks.

00:43:34.311 --> 00:43:36.520
Then, I could show
you a large number

00:43:36.521 --> 00:43:38.975
of other plots that you
can find in the paper.

00:43:38.976 --> 00:43:42.785
I guess that's probably
not appropriate here,

00:43:42.786 --> 00:43:44.750
and I want to wrap up briefly

00:43:44.751 --> 00:43:47.360
with a couple of
takeaway messages.

00:43:47.361 --> 00:43:48.960
So, first takeaway
message is that

00:43:48.961 --> 00:43:51.525
we now have the lowest
diameter topology,

00:43:51.526 --> 00:43:52.920
in the sense that we
now approach the Moore

00:43:52.921 --> 00:43:54.705
bound and it's going
to be very hard,

00:43:54.706 --> 00:43:57.765
impossible to improve upon
the cost of this topology.

00:43:57.766 --> 00:43:59.810
Because, well,
the theoretical bound,

00:43:59.811 --> 00:44:01.245
that we cannot take
make much better,

00:44:01.246 --> 00:44:03.010
and it is resilient.

00:44:03.011 --> 00:44:05.830
It's very cost and
power effective.

00:44:05.831 --> 00:44:07.750
A 25 percent over Dragonfly

00:44:07.751 --> 00:44:11.590
and 26 percent in terms of power.

00:44:11.680 --> 00:44:14.670
It gives you the lowest latency
because diameter two and

00:44:14.671 --> 00:44:16.700
it gives you
a full global bandwidth,

00:44:16.701 --> 00:44:18.845
and then we have lots
of results for this.

00:44:18.846 --> 00:44:20.985
One last thing I
wanted to mention,

00:44:20.986 --> 00:44:23.235
you can actually use
that same topology

00:44:23.236 --> 00:44:25.145
to build a network on chip.

00:44:25.146 --> 00:44:28.340
It's exactly the same,
very nice properties.

00:44:28.341 --> 00:44:29.410
But of course in
a different setting,

00:44:29.411 --> 00:44:30.340
we can never go in chips,

00:44:30.341 --> 00:44:31.400
it had different parameters,

00:44:31.401 --> 00:44:33.785
they don't have fiber
connections, for example.

00:44:33.786 --> 00:44:36.890
But we did this, this appeared
this year at [inaudible].

00:44:36.891 --> 00:44:38.760
But it's basically
the same construction,

00:44:38.761 --> 00:44:39.730
it's slightly different layout

00:44:39.731 --> 00:44:41.040
because we need to lay it out in

00:44:41.041 --> 00:44:43.330
a 2D book graph style topology,

00:44:43.331 --> 00:44:44.745
so limited number of layers,

00:44:44.746 --> 00:44:46.060
but this works really,

00:44:46.061 --> 00:44:47.810
really well in a network
on chip as well,

00:44:47.811 --> 00:44:48.960
and we assume that there are many

00:44:48.961 --> 00:44:50.165
different other uses for this.

00:44:50.166 --> 00:44:51.890
So, with that, I would
like to thank you for

00:44:51.891 --> 00:44:54.680
your attention and I'm
open for questions.

00:44:58.590 --> 00:45:00.895
Any questions that
haven't been asked

00:45:00.896 --> 00:45:02.995
during the talk? Yes.

00:45:02.996 --> 00:45:05.930
&gt;&gt; So, do you have
any intuition while you

00:45:05.931 --> 00:45:09.810
are making that graph
using those finite fields.

00:45:10.390 --> 00:45:13.830
&gt;&gt; So, yes but it took us a

00:45:13.831 --> 00:45:16.625
while to develop
that intuition why we do this.

00:45:16.626 --> 00:45:18.720
Eventually, it is really

00:45:18.721 --> 00:45:21.375
a graph theoretical problem
that you want to solve.

00:45:21.376 --> 00:45:23.630
In the nice pieces
that this problem,

00:45:23.631 --> 00:45:25.080
this degree-diameter problem and

00:45:25.081 --> 00:45:26.310
you should really Google for it,

00:45:26.311 --> 00:45:29.805
it's very interesting for
theoretical mathematicians.

00:45:29.806 --> 00:45:32.405
So there has been
quite some work on this.

00:45:32.406 --> 00:45:35.570
The idea why we go through

00:45:35.571 --> 00:45:37.340
these finite fields or

00:45:37.341 --> 00:45:38.850
why they go through
these finite fields,

00:45:38.851 --> 00:45:44.020
it's really to build the
graph with the maximum girth.

00:45:44.021 --> 00:45:46.960
So the girth is the length of

00:45:46.961 --> 00:45:52.720
the shortest length
of the circle.

00:45:52.721 --> 00:45:54.520
We want to maximize this
because you want to

00:45:54.521 --> 00:45:57.210
distribute data as
much as possible,

00:45:57.211 --> 00:45:58.850
as quickly as possible
to as many nodes.

00:45:58.851 --> 00:46:00.490
If you have many short circles,

00:46:00.491 --> 00:46:02.820
I mean trivial circles,

00:46:02.821 --> 00:46:05.240
then this is not
suitable too well.

00:46:05.241 --> 00:46:08.550
So, that's my intuition
that I developed over time.

00:46:08.551 --> 00:46:10.720
You could also look
at Ramanujan graphs,

00:46:10.721 --> 00:46:12.570
and then there are
other graph classes

00:46:12.571 --> 00:46:15.730
that don't only work
for the diameter two,

00:46:15.731 --> 00:46:17.775
but they're further away
from the Moore bound.

00:46:17.776 --> 00:46:20.110
So, for diameter two, we
are very lucky that we have

00:46:20.111 --> 00:46:22.290
this one construction
that gets us very,

00:46:22.291 --> 00:46:27.190
very close, and for the other
larger diameters we don't.

00:46:27.191 --> 00:46:28.590
But for larger diameter, we have

00:46:28.591 --> 00:46:30.090
different topologies
that we could use.

00:46:30.091 --> 00:46:37.200
So, that base is covered
well. Any other questions?

00:46:37.201 --> 00:46:41.855
&gt;&gt; Does the routing needs to
be optimized to know that-?

00:46:41.856 --> 00:46:44.770
&gt;&gt; Yes, yes, I skipped
the routing slides,

00:46:44.771 --> 00:46:48.320
but you can actually use
the same by construction,

00:46:48.321 --> 00:46:49.895
you know where the cables are.

00:46:49.896 --> 00:46:53.565
And by construction, you
can find the shortest path,

00:46:53.566 --> 00:46:56.310
in this three-dimensional space
that are lined out,

00:46:56.311 --> 00:46:59.230
you can find
which node to hop to,

00:46:59.231 --> 00:47:01.415
where to go to the final
destination, but that's not all.

00:47:01.416 --> 00:47:02.975
Routing is slightly
more complicated.

00:47:02.976 --> 00:47:04.340
You cannot really employ

00:47:04.341 --> 00:47:06.400
shortest path routing
on this topology.

00:47:06.401 --> 00:47:08.620
I had to skip this
for time reasons.

00:47:08.621 --> 00:47:11.665
Well, you can. Okay, so
let me be more precise.

00:47:11.666 --> 00:47:13.370
You can apply
shortest path routing,

00:47:13.371 --> 00:47:15.440
where you just route along
the shortest path which is

00:47:15.441 --> 00:47:17.770
always of diameter two, right?

00:47:17.771 --> 00:47:19.050
But if you do this,

00:47:19.051 --> 00:47:21.445
then I can give you
a traffic pattern

00:47:21.446 --> 00:47:23.345
where you will look really bad.

00:47:23.346 --> 00:47:27.140
So, I will just choose
some nodes and I will

00:47:27.141 --> 00:47:31.030
create a congestion that
is of the order of k. So,

00:47:31.031 --> 00:47:33.330
if k is zero degree.

00:47:33.331 --> 00:47:35.230
This is very easy to
construct actually,

00:47:35.231 --> 00:47:36.690
so it's not too hard.

00:47:36.691 --> 00:47:37.830
So, if you don't want to run

00:47:37.831 --> 00:47:39.330
into this problem,
what you have to do,

00:47:39.331 --> 00:47:42.490
is you have to employ
adaptive routing.

00:47:42.491 --> 00:47:44.085
So, you have to somehow check,

00:47:44.086 --> 00:47:45.950
well how busy is my next link,

00:47:45.951 --> 00:47:47.450
and if that link is
very busy because

00:47:47.451 --> 00:47:50.310
already too many endpoints
are sending onto that link,

00:47:50.311 --> 00:47:53.945
I'm sending along
a non-minimal path, right?

00:47:53.946 --> 00:47:56.445
So, and in the results,

00:47:56.446 --> 00:47:58.140
I skipped this for time reasons,

00:47:58.141 --> 00:47:59.760
but I can talk a little bit.

00:47:59.761 --> 00:48:00.770
So, here we have
different routing,

00:48:00.771 --> 00:48:02.010
and this is minimum routing.

00:48:02.011 --> 00:48:03.935
This is what I just said,
there's no adaptivity,

00:48:03.936 --> 00:48:08.740
and not looking at
the state of the network,

00:48:08.741 --> 00:48:10.415
so it's completely oblivious.

00:48:10.416 --> 00:48:13.820
Then, we have a valiant routing
which is also oblivious,

00:48:13.821 --> 00:48:15.315
but what valiant routing does.,

00:48:15.316 --> 00:48:17.660
is it picks a random other node,

00:48:17.661 --> 00:48:19.380
we're routing to
this random other node,

00:48:19.381 --> 00:48:21.550
and then this random other node
runs to the destination.

00:48:21.551 --> 00:48:23.260
It doesn't do too well

00:48:23.261 --> 00:48:25.000
because we're losing
half the bandwidth,

00:48:25.001 --> 00:48:28.160
you can also see that it's
asymptotically approaching

00:48:28.161 --> 00:48:31.590
50 percent because we now
double the path length, okay?

00:48:31.591 --> 00:48:32.925
But that's one of these things.

00:48:32.926 --> 00:48:34.370
Then, we can use
this UGAL routing,

00:48:34.371 --> 00:48:37.815
which was also developed at
Stanford, which gives us,

00:48:37.816 --> 00:48:39.130
there are two different variants

00:48:39.131 --> 00:48:40.550
here, no actually, this is L,

00:48:40.551 --> 00:48:44.010
which gives us
a very good result as well.

00:48:44.011 --> 00:48:48.975
And UGAL is now
Uniform Global Adaptive,

00:48:48.976 --> 00:48:51.565
and this L is for local.

00:48:51.566 --> 00:48:55.140
Uniformly Global Adaptive Load
balanced dash local,

00:48:55.141 --> 00:48:56.430
there is a global one as well.

00:48:56.431 --> 00:48:58.640
Local only assumes
local knowledge of

00:48:58.641 --> 00:49:00.925
the switch, global
is unreasonable,

00:49:00.926 --> 00:49:02.220
because it assumes that you know

00:49:02.221 --> 00:49:04.070
all the other switches
states in the network

00:49:04.071 --> 00:49:05.110
when making a routing decision,

00:49:05.111 --> 00:49:06.240
which of course you don't.

00:49:06.241 --> 00:49:07.910
But they use this in the thesis

00:49:07.911 --> 00:49:10.285
of the student just to
get the upper bound,

00:49:10.286 --> 00:49:11.705
I mean what if we would know,

00:49:11.706 --> 00:49:13.165
this is why we didn't consider.

00:49:13.166 --> 00:49:15.175
So, this is the adaptive
routing that we would

00:49:15.176 --> 00:49:18.000
suggest using four Slim Fly.

00:49:18.001 --> 00:49:20.680
In fact, a very similar routing

00:49:20.681 --> 00:49:23.060
is necessary for Dragonfly,

00:49:24.110 --> 00:49:26.840
this is why we only have
one result of Dragonfly

00:49:26.841 --> 00:49:29.020
by default uses that loading.

00:49:29.021 --> 00:49:32.350
Any of my conjecture would be,

00:49:32.351 --> 00:49:35.890
that any low diameter
topology that is

00:49:35.891 --> 00:49:39.920
cost-effective will need
adaptive routing, right?

00:49:39.921 --> 00:49:42.370
Fat Tree also needs it,

00:49:42.371 --> 00:49:46.470
but you can get around it,
most infant event networks,

00:49:46.471 --> 00:49:48.225
Fat Tree or route it statically.

00:49:48.226 --> 00:49:50.715
&gt;&gt; On the adaptive ones,
do you know,

00:49:50.716 --> 00:49:52.515
well this is the closer,

00:49:52.516 --> 00:49:55.080
but then this one
is also close or?

00:49:55.081 --> 00:49:55.440
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:49:55.441 --> 00:49:58.890
&gt;&gt; You go probably from shortest
to longest in that case.

00:49:58.891 --> 00:50:00.465
&gt;&gt; Correct, what
you typically have,

00:50:00.466 --> 00:50:02.155
is you have a table,

00:50:02.156 --> 00:50:04.255
you look at your output,
your length.

00:50:04.256 --> 00:50:05.610
So, you look at what's

00:50:05.611 --> 00:50:07.985
the traffic jam into
that direction.

00:50:07.986 --> 00:50:09.900
If it's too much, then I have

00:50:09.901 --> 00:50:13.260
a secondary and tertiary options

00:50:13.261 --> 00:50:14.385
where you just move to next one.

00:50:14.386 --> 00:50:15.655
Check if that's too long,

00:50:15.656 --> 00:50:16.980
and if that's too
long then I go to

00:50:16.981 --> 00:50:18.520
the next one and so on
and thereby distance.

00:50:18.521 --> 00:50:19.940
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:50:19.941 --> 00:50:20.150
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:50:20.151 --> 00:50:20.890
&gt;&gt; The best part.

00:50:20.891 --> 00:50:22.575
&gt;&gt; They're about
five papers how to

00:50:22.576 --> 00:50:24.930
optimize this for Slim Fly by

00:50:24.931 --> 00:50:29.360
a Spanish group that has
specialized on routing.

00:50:29.361 --> 00:50:31.505
So, we have just used
the standard routing,

00:50:31.506 --> 00:50:32.860
but they're actually building

00:50:32.861 --> 00:50:34.270
a real technology out of this.

00:50:34.271 --> 00:50:37.140
It's also not so
trivial because again,

00:50:37.141 --> 00:50:38.440
you need to have
all these streaks and

00:50:38.441 --> 00:50:39.860
actually get your packets
through the network,

00:50:39.861 --> 00:50:41.180
then it's also
virtual channel question.

00:50:41.181 --> 00:50:43.095
How many virtual
channels do you need,

00:50:43.096 --> 00:50:44.940
that you don't have deadlocks?

00:50:44.941 --> 00:50:47.000
This much more complicated than I

00:50:47.001 --> 00:50:49.465
could explain in
this 40 minute talk.

00:50:49.466 --> 00:50:52.130
But actually, quite
many other groups have

00:50:52.131 --> 00:50:55.510
